WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:05.540
Phillip: Organisations have employment policies
and procedures that exist to help team leaders

00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:10.610
and managers, and, in this video, we are going
to be looking at how these can help guide a

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:12.670
manager in dealing with disciplinary issues.

00:00:13.719 --> 00:00:17.469
Maddy: Although it can often be best
if appropriate, to address disciplinary

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:23.320
issues informally at first, more serious
issues need to be addressed formally and

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:28.400
organisations should have clearly defined
policies and procedures for this in place,

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:30.929
to make sure everyone is treated fairly.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:35.800
Organisations that don’t follow
policies and procedures put themselves

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:38.130
at risk of breaking the law.

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:41.620
Phillip: There are two categories of problem
that disciplinary procedures are designed

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:45.190
to respond to Misconduct and Capability.

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:49.679
Misconduct occurs when someone
is behaving inappropriately or

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:51.739
against the organisation’s values

00:00:52.459 --> 00:00:56.120
Maddy: Capability issues are to do with
not working to the standard that the

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:58.339
organisation expects from its employees

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:02.089
So, what should a disciplinary procedure include?

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:06.670
• Phillip: A meeting with the employee to tell
them that the disciplinary procedure is underway

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:09.470
• Maddy: An investigation to
find out what’s going on

00:01:10.289 --> 00:01:13.450
• Phillip: An option to suspend
the person involved, if necessary

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:17.809
• Maddy: A formal disciplinary hearing, which
will be a meeting of everyone involved

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:22.600
• Phillip: If necessary, a sanction against
the person, which can include being dismissed

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.500
• Maddy: An option to appeal the
decision and an appeal meeting

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:29.860
• Phillip: Final confirmation of
the decision that’s been made

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:35.070
Maddy: And, throughout this process, the person
in question has the right to be accompanied

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:37.460
by a colleague or a union representative.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:43.249
Phillip: This is the minimum disciplinary
policy an employer should have, but what other

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:48.619
policies can an employer have, that can help a
team leader or manager with disciplinary issues?

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:50.340
Maddy: Timekeeping

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:56.870
Having a formal policy around timekeeping can
be useful to prevent absenteeism, when employees

00:01:56.889 --> 00:01:59.440
take time off without permission or a good reason.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:04.969
Typically, a timekeeping policy would mean
that most employees are required to accurately

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:07.359
log the time they arrive and leave work.

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:12.450
They may also have to log the time that they
spend on individual projects or pieces of work.

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:17.260
This can make it easier to spot
absenteeism and makes it less likely

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:19.070
that people will be absent without leave.

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:20.999
Phillip: Conduct Policy

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.350
This formally describes the conduct that
an employer expects from its employees.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:31.320
Typically, it will state that employees
are expected to follow the organisation’s

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:36.819
values, treat each other respectfully,
and not bully, harass, or discriminate.

00:02:37.429 --> 00:02:42.610
It may also have rules specific to the
organisation, like defining how equipment

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:47.600
should be treated, and attitudes expected
when dealing with external clients, and so on.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:53.549
Maddy: This helps show what behaviour is
acceptable or unacceptable and is also a useful

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:58.660
reference for a team leader as to when and if it’s
necessary to take disciplinary actions or not.

00:02:59.449 --> 00:03:00.569
Phillip: Gross misconduct

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:06.000
There are some actions, like theft or
physical violence, where an employee can

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.709
be dismissed immediately, however, these
still need to be investigated properly

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:12.980
giving an employee chance to respond.

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:17.820
This allows for a quick response to
very serious disciplinary issues.

00:03:18.549 --> 00:03:19.870
Maddy: Capability policy

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:24.610
This defines the standard of work that an
organisation expects from its employees

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:28.230
and gives a framework for responding
to the problem of underperforming.

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:33.969
These policies should be focused on identifying
capability issues and giving the employee

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:36.450
opportunities to develop and improve.

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:41.450
They should also support the employee
so their performance can be improved and

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:43.310
provide them with training where necessary.

00:03:44.570 --> 00:03:48.339
Phillip: By having policies like this
to refer to and in place, you should be

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:51.970
in a better position as a team leader
to respond to various disciplinary

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.310
issues before they become too serious.

